31:| src/lsp.rs | +183 −29 | round-1 review fixes: a `set` reuses only variable-like slots (variable, parameter, loop variable, catch), so a later `set` after a constant, function, import, object or module starts a fresh variable binding; a called name only falls back to a function declared after the scope's current entry, so a call after a shadowing `set` resolves to nothing; and a method is call-only, so a bare method name answers no lexical binding while `name()` still resolves the method. |
35:| src/lsp.rs | +108 −1 | resumed run: member completion. The analysis keeps its object field/method/parent tables and the `this`-to-object map, and `member_completions` answers a `receiver.member` slot with the object's own and inherited members or a standard module's published members, filtered by the partial member name. The ordinary lexical answer still runs for every position that is not a member slot. |
37:| src/lsp.rs | +65 −32 | round-1 review fixes for member completion: the analysis records an import name or alias to the module it names, mirroring the runtime's `module_aliases`, so `import files as f` followed by `f.` looks up `MODULE_MEMBERS` for `files`; and completion recognises a syntactic member slot before resolving the receiver, then answers from the complete walk, so `Person.` above `object Person` offers the object's members instead of falling back to keywords. A member slot whose receiver is neither an object nor a standard module now answers empty rather than offering the lexical registry. |
38:| tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs | +63 −0 | two round-1 review tests: an aliased module member slot offers its published members and no keywords, and an object member slot above the object's declaration offers its field and method and no keywords. |
41:| src/lsp.rs | +39 −7 | resumed run: goto-definition and find-references follow a call to a program-declared module's published function. Each module body's top-level `to` records its binding, the module's last `export` filters the published functions when the body closes, and the deferred member pass maps `Module.name()` and `alias.name()` to that declaration. An unexported name and a standard module's member stay unresolved, matching the runtime. |
43:| src/lsp.rs | +30 −9 | final round-1 review fixes: the published-function table is keyed by the module binding rather than its name, so a redeclaration or a same-named module in another block cannot answer for the first module's calls, and a module declaration gives a redeclaration its own binding; a module-top-level `set` removes the same-named function entry because it replaced the member value (the round-3 review corrected the `constant` half: a module `constant` binds a program global and leaves the function published); and a bare `Module.name` read resolves through the same table as a call, matching the runtime's exposed function value. |
44:| tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs | +130 −0 | four final round-1 review tests: two same-named module declarations keep their own functions; a `set` that shadows a module function does not navigate to the stale `to` (the `constant` half was corrected in round 3); and a bare module-member read resolves to the published function and joins its references. |
51:| src/lsp.rs | +260 −0 | resumed run and round-1 review: completion inside a `{…}` text interpolation. The literal is decoded first, so `\u007B`/`\u007D` are braces to the LSP exactly as they are to the lexer; a pair is interpolation only when its contents re-parse as one complete expression, so a JSON object in a string stays text; `this` is offered as an expression keyword; and a `receiver.member` slot inside a pair is re-analyzed in the literal's enclosing scopes so it answers the object's or module's members rather than a flat lexical list. Text outside a pair, and a closed pair, still answer nothing. |
